#{[17592186134370 "Using $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F=\\langle R_y-Q_z,P_z-R_x,Q_x-P_y\\rangle$ gives\n$$\\nabla\\times\\langle xy,yz,xz\\rangle=\\langle-y,-z,-x\\rangle.$$"] [17592186134371 "The source asks for curl. For $\\mathbf F=\\langle x\\cos(yz),0,y\\ln(xz)\\rangle$, where $xz>0$,\n$$\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\ln(xz),-xy\\sin(yz)-\\frac yx,xz\\sin(yz)\\right\\rangle.$$\nAt $(1,\\pi/2,1)$ this becomes $\\langle0,-\\pi,1\\rangle$."] [17592186134376 "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}2t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-12{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{{u}^{3}|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =4({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134377 "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}t},\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}t}⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134378 "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}-3\\operatorname{sin}t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{6{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134386 "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\hspace{1em} & {\\mathbf{r}}_{1}^{'}(t)=4t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}-3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\hspace{1em} & {\\mathbf{r}}_{2}^{'}(t)=\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}y\\,dx-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-{t}^{3})\\cdot 4t\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & =4[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186152727 "Parametrize by $x=t$, $y=t^2/2$, $0\\le t\\le2$. Along the path, $y/x=t/2$ away from the origin and has limit $0$ there. Also $ds=\\sqrt{1+t^2}\\,dt$. Hence the integral, interpreted with this removable endpoint limit, is\n$$\\frac12\\int_0^2t\\sqrt{1+t^2}\\,dt.$$"] [17592186152728 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)={e}^{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le e\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+({e}^{t})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=5y{e}^{-x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =5{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{e}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{e}5\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{e}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186152745 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)={e}^{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{t}⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}3\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2({e}^{t})^{2}=2{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}3}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}3}2{e}^{2t}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}3}2{e}^{2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+{e}^{2t}$, $\\text{d}u=2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{10}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{2}^{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({10}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(10\\sqrt{10}-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}(5\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186152746 "Set $x=t$, $y=2t+1$, $0\\le t\\le1$. Then $x(y-1)=2t^2$ and $ds=\\sqrt5\\,dt$. Consequently\n$$\\int_0^1 2\\sqrt5\\,t^2\\,dt=\\frac{2\\sqrt5}{3}.$$"] [17592186157616 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $2$ and $3$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ we obtain $r=3.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}2\\le r\\le 3,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{r}^{4}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{r}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[-\\frac{1}{2{r}^{2}}]}_{2}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{18})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{72}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{72}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{72}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186157838 "The annulus has $1\\le r\\le3$ and $0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$. Multiplication by the polar Jacobian gives $(1-r^2)r$, so use\n$$\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_1^3r(1-r^2)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.$$"] [17592186157848 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186167049 "A vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{xy})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{F}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$"] [17592186167050 "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ be a vector field with domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf{F}$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ the domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=(x-a){e}^{x+y},\\,Q=x{e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\n \nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=(x-a){e}^{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=(x+1){e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a){e}^{x+y} & =(x+1){e}^{x+y} \\\\ (x-a){e}^{x+y}-(x+1){e}^{x+y} & =0 \\\\ (x-a-x-1){e}^{x+y} & =0 \\\\ -(a+1){e}^{x+y} & =0 \\\\ a & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-1.$"] [17592186167090 "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ be a vector field with domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf{F}$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ the domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=2ay,\\,Q=3-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2a,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a & =-1 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [17592186167541 "A vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2xyz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{x}^{2}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{F}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=2xyz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{x}^{2}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\n$$"] [17592186172280 "The function $\\mathbf{F}$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}(x,y)\\ne (0,0)\\}.\n$$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\pm x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}y\\ne \\pm x\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}|\\hspace{0.222em}y\\ne \\pm x\\}\\hspace{0.167em}.\n$$"] [17592186172281 "The function $\\mathbf{F}$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the interior of a circle of radius $3,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/e5/e54618f7e72d70f299686ca2846a1d6fa0caccb8daecb090b3a653271c88fcd3.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is the entire plane ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ with the origin removed:\n\n![](images/5b/5b77ad4e9574e94e6a05eee7680f4b57d0190d40583c37c2097acef958cab7e2.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/30/30462ed1f25749ac4ef05cf9ca9573508ae28a0799d7986dd005f71fdac15679.png)"] [17592186172301 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,0)$ and $B(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2).$\n\nNotice that $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ with domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y{e}^{x{y}^{2}}(1+x{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{ln}2,2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}2\\cdot {2}^{2}}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}2}-1 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}{2}^{4}}-1 \\\\  & ={2}^{4}-1 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186172302 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,2)$ and $B(1,-3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(1,-3)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =(4(1)-3(-3))-(4(0)-3(2)) \\\\  & =4+9+6 \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186172303 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,0)$ and $B(\\sqrt{\\pi },1).$\n\nNotice that $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ with domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathrm{∇}f=\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{\\pi },1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(1)\\operatorname{cos}(\\sqrt{\\pi })^{2}-(0)\\operatorname{cos}{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\\pi -0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186172306 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6437eddec7d44a10c7fb2b3461a09844ca06e5131b69503f51432eb87fedd31.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{y}+2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{x}+2x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy+4xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}8xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{-1}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4(2)^{2}y-4(-1)^{2}y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(16y-4y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}12y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =6{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186172307 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/37/3786d0a5fb99d1a32d761e067f9c49b6b7da05a11fc555dd94a4ef42d753f129.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)-{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2x+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-2x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[2x-{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(2-1)-0]\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =y\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186172308 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/c3/c3c617522488e6ebf47dd2b2a52c43a1fe06ccaef8cd1e8752ab4df5e2d4bd64.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+1$ and $Q(x,y)={y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2(x+y).$"] [17592186172309 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/26/265d27ad87a7f114bd2a32a5f48cbfedb13192abf48f9b759c4ed635e71ff144.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=5xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+5x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2y+5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186172675 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=f(\\mathbf{r}(b))-f(\\mathbf{r}(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(1,0,-1)$ and $B(2,1,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & =f(2,1,1)-f(1,0,-1) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1+{1}^{2})-(1\\cdot 0\\cdot (-1)+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186176231 "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}x+(4-x{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/09/09a69771617d73af6e4a1a42720223951e2e6edeb71a8df4d12aeb1bc22031aa.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,Q(x,y)=4-x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx+(4-x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-{y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}-({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-4[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-4(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =-8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186176312 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/70/709d108b2b992d1714c7c0b3bef26f52e965c2467f38d05103cd45f4f9bc821b.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}-4{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)={e}^{y}+4x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\sqrt{x}-4{x}^{2}y)dx+({e}^{y}+4x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4{y}^{2}-(-4{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}4({r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{r}^{4}]_{0}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}16\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16[\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186176313 "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}x+{y}^{3})\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}x+(y{e}^{y+1}+x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/3b/3b90abecc774b5ba5dcee19385fef38b14206d2c31c3b6cba38216bfa9957448.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=y{e}^{y+1}+x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3})\\,dx-(y{e}^{y+1}+x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-3{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}(1-3\\cdot {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\hspace{0.222em}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-3{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186180030 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $5,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+5\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the double-angle formula for cosine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}t)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}t)-(5)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }5{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+5\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(2t)+1}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={5(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(2t)}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =5(0+\\pi -1)-5(0-1) \\\\  & =5\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186180038 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf{T}(t).$\n\nIn this case, $\\mathbf{n}$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $\\mathbf{T},$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](images/e9/e90c36e34c6ab51f15707685a22c8bd2c749b9857ca24c59631f09dfbda495c6.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =y={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t)\\cdot (2t)-({t}^{2})\\cdot (1)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186180039 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf{T}(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y=t,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,2.)$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{n}$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $\\mathbf{T},$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/b7/b7ac1ce4a79528f5660b8f898739da72489ebe8e8fc156020efae77a103fbec4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =2, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2})=\\frac{1}{2}t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2)\\cdot (1)-(-4)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2+2t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2t+{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 2+{2}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186180040 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x+y=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t), \\\\ Q(x,y) & =y-x=3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3(\\operatorname{cos}t+\\operatorname{sin}t))\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}t)-(3(\\operatorname{sin}t-\\operatorname{cos}t))\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t-\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =9(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =18\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186180041 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $3$ and $2,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{2}=4{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =xy=6\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}t)-(6\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\operatorname{cos}t+18{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }26{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{1}26{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186198594 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨z+y,\\hspace{0.222em}2y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩{|}_{z=x-y} \\\\  & =⟨(x-y)+y,\\hspace{0.222em}2y+(x-y),\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{x}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{x+y}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{xy}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z+y,\\hspace{0.222em}2y+z,\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x)\\cdot 1-(x+y)\\cdot (-1)+xy)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x+x+y+xy)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+xy)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+xy)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+2x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2x+{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2(1)+{1}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186198602 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,y)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'} & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(x,y))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({\\color{blue}-P}\\frac{{\\color{blue}∂g}}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{{\\color{blue}∂y}}+R)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186198605 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,y)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'} & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(x,y))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{x}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{y}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({\\color{blue}-P}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}+({\\color{blue}-Q}\\hspace{0.167em})\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186198610 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v)=⟨u,\\hspace{0.222em}v,\\hspace{0.222em}u+v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}⟩{|}_{x=u,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,y=v,\\hspace{0.167em}\\,z=u+v} \\\\  & =⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(u+v)^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{v}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(u+v)^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot (-1)+{v}^{2}\\cdot (-1)+(u+v)^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}+{u}^{2}+2uv+{v}^{2} \\\\  & =2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{x}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2uv)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\color{blue}2uv}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186198615 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x-y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨{e}^{x},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{y},\\hspace{0.222em}z+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨{e}^{x},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{y},\\hspace{0.222em}z+1⟩{|}_{z=-x-y} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{{e}^{x}}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{{e}^{y}}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{1-x-y}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨{e}^{x},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{y},\\hspace{0.222em}z+1⟩\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-({e}^{x})\\cdot (-1)-({e}^{y})\\cdot (-1)+1-x-y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+1-x-y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+1-x-y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{e}^{x}y+{e}^{y}+y-xy-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}+e+1-x-\\frac{1}{2}-1)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}-x-\\frac{1}{2}+e)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{x}{2}+ex]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =e-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}+e-1 \\\\  & =2e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186233174 "We apply the power rule with $n=20$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{20}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{20+1}}{20+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{21}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{21})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{21}\\cdot 21{x}^{20} \\\\ &= {x}^{20}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235522 "If $\\mathbf{V}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{V}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{V}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{V}=\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy) \\\\  & =2xy+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V}$ at the point $(1,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{V}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{(1,\\hspace{0.167em}1)} & =2(1)(1)+2(1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235527 "If $\\mathbf{V}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{V}$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $\\mathbf{V}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $P={x}^{2}y$ and $Q=2xy.$ Therefore, the scalar curl (i.e., the vorticity) of $\\mathbf{V}$ at $(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\omega  & =\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =2y-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(1,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\omega (1,1) & =(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & =2(1)-(1)^{2} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235528 "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ be a vector field with domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n>$\\mathbf{F}$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor our vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ the domain is $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=a{y}^{3}-1,\\,Q=3x{y}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3a{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a{y}^{2} & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ 3a & =3 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=1.$"] [17592186235529 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn part 1, we found that $a=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=({y}^{3}-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x{y}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =0+6xy \\\\  & =6xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235533 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the disk enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le r\\le 3,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235535 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0+2x+0 \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235537 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nIn part 1, we found that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} &=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}2x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}2x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186235539 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\times \\mathbf{F}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ {z}^{2} & 2xy & x{y}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xy)]\\mathbf{i}-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2})]\\mathbf{j}+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})]\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2xy-0)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-({y}^{2}-2z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(2y-0)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-({y}^{2}-2z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2xy,\\hspace{0.222em}2z-{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}2y⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186237924 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x \\\\ y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-5\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=y+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${x}^{2}+(y+5)^{2}=1.$"] [17592186237925 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x-3}{5} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y+2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{x-3}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y+2}{3}\\right)}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\left(\\frac{x-3}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y+2}{3}\\right)}^{2}=1.\n$$"] [17592186237926 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=x \\\\ y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t & =1 \\\\ {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t & =1-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}t & =\\sqrt{1-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, using the definition of a tangent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}t=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t}}{\\operatorname{cos}t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{x}.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{x}.$"] [17592186238016 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2x & 3 & 2 \\\\ x & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 2 \\\\ x & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 3 \\\\ x & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(-3-2)\\mathbf{i}-(-2x-2x)\\mathbf{j}+(2x-3x)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-5,-4x,x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-4x)^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+16{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17{x}^{2}+25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{93} \\\\ \\sqrt{17{x}^{2}+25} & =\\sqrt{93} \\\\ 17{x}^{2}+25 & =93 \\\\ 17{x}^{2} & =68 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\pm 2.$"] [17592186238017 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2x\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2x\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(4x-0)\\mathbf{i}-(-6x-0)\\mathbf{j}+(12-4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨4x,6x,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{(4x)^{2}+(6x)^{2}+(8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+36{x}^{2}+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{52{x}^{2}+64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{77} \\\\ \\sqrt{52{x}^{2}+64} & =\\sqrt{77} \\\\ 52{x}^{2}+64 & =77 \\\\ 52{x}^{2} & =13 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.$"] [17592186238018 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & 2x & 3 \\\\ 2 & -x & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 3 \\\\ -x & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2x \\\\ 2 & -x\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2x+3x)\\mathbf{i}-(-1-6)\\mathbf{j}+(x-4x)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨5x,7,-3x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{(5x)^{2}+(7)^{2}+(-3x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{x}^{2}+49+9{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34{x}^{2}+49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{355} \\\\ \\sqrt{34{x}^{2}+49} & =\\sqrt{355} \\\\ 34{x}^{2}+49 & =355 \\\\ 34{x}^{2} & =306 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\pm 3.$"] [17592186238021 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 4 & x & 3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}x & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & x \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0-9)\\mathbf{i}-(0+6)\\mathbf{j}+(12+2x)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-9,-6,12+2x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{(-9)^{2}+(-6)^{2}+(12+2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81+36+144+48x+4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{261+48x+4{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{181} \\\\ \\sqrt{261+48x+4{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{181} \\\\ 261+48x+4{x}^{2} & =181 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+48x+80 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+12x+20 & =0 \\\\ (x+10)(x+2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-10,\\,-2.$"] [17592186238022 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -x & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -x & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -x\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(6+4x)\\mathbf{i}-(0+4)\\mathbf{j}+(0+3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨6+4x,-4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{(6+4x)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+48x+16{x}^{2}+16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{61+48x+16{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{221} \\\\ \\sqrt{61+48x+16{x}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{221} \\\\ 61+48x+16{x}^{2} & =221 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}+48x-160 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x-10 & =0 \\\\ (x+5)(x-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-5,\\,2.$"] [17592186238024 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2x\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2x\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(4x-3)\\mathbf{i}-(0+6)\\mathbf{j}+(0-4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨4x-3,-6,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{(4x-3)^{2}+(-6)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-24x+9+36+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-24x+61}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{53} \\\\ \\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-24x+61} & =\\sqrt{53} \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-24x+61 & =53 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-24x+8 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3x+1 & =0 \\\\ (2x-1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2},\\hspace{0.167em}1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,1.$"] [17592186240865 "A vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{F}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}.\n$$"] [17592186240866 "A vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{F}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$"] [17592186240867 "A vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}(4x-3y))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}(4x-3y))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}(4x-3y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3\\operatorname{cos}(4x-3y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{F}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=4\\operatorname{cos}(4x-3y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3\\operatorname{cos}(4x-3y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$"] [17592186240870 "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ be a vector field with domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf{F}$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ we have $P=ay-2x{y}^{3}$ and $Q=x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=a-6x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1-6x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-6x{y}^{2} & =1-6x{y}^{2} \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=1.$"] [17592186240872 "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ be a vector field with domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf{F}$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ we have $P=\\frac{axy}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $Q=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{ax}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{ax}{{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ ax & =2x \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=2.$"] [17592186241078 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{sin}x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}y) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{cos}x-2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}y\\operatorname{sin}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186241107 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\times \\mathbf{F}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 3{z}^{2} & 2y & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y)]\\mathbf{i}-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{z}^{2})]\\mathbf{j}+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{z}^{2})]\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0-0)\\mathbf{i}-(0-6z)\\mathbf{j}+(0-0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =6z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186241111 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\times \\mathbf{F}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ {e}^{y+z} & 1 & xyz\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(1)]\\mathbf{i}-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{y+z})]\\mathbf{j}+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y+z})]\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(xz-0)\\mathbf{i}-(yz-{e}^{y+z})\\mathbf{j}+(0-{e}^{y+z})\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({e}^{y+z}-yz)\\mathbf{j}-{e}^{y+z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(2,1,-1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F})(2,1,-1) & =2(-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({e}^{1+(-1)}-1(-1))\\mathbf{j}-{e}^{1+(-1)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186241157 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(x+1) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{9}{(x+1)}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2 & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(y+2) & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{9}{(y+2)}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9}{(x+1)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{(y+2)}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ 4{(x+1)}^{2}+4{(y+2)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation of the circle is ${(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}.$"] [17592186241158 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) & =5\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+\\frac{1}{2}) & =5\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(y+\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $$\n\\displaystyle {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25.\n$$"] [17592186243923 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={(\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\frac{25}{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}.$"] [17592186253570 "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta })+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =1+({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta })+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =2.\n$$"] [17592186253571 "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{5}\\theta ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta =1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta ) & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta \\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{5}\\theta ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186253572 "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186253574 "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}\\theta\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta =1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot 1+\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186253576 "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }=\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\cdot 1+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{csc}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{csc}\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186253577 "First, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{5}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }=\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta \\cdot ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta \\cdot ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )} & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta \\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{5}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cot}\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186253578 "First, we factor $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ from the first two terms of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x+\\operatorname{sin}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}x+1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)+1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)+1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot 1+1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x+1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}+\\frac{1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}x}+\\frac{1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{sin}x}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{sin}x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}x}+\\frac{1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}x}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}x+{\\operatorname{csc}}^{2}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x+\\operatorname{sin}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}x+1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}=\\operatorname{cot}x+{\\operatorname{csc}}^{2}x.\n$$"] [17592186253594 "First, we rewrite the first term using the definition of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x}-\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}x)\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x-(\\operatorname{cos}x-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x-\\operatorname{cos}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the Pythagorean identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x-\\operatorname{cos}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x} & =\\frac{({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)-\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$"] [17592186253596 "We put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }+\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the Pythagorean identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\beg